B. EFFICIENCY-BASED CONSIDERATIONS
1. Adverse Selection
As noted above, the risk of adverse selection is one potential efficiency cost of legal restrictions on insurers' risk-classification practices. Indeed, the risk of adverse selection is so often associated with regulatory restrictions on risk classification that some refer to the phenomenon as "regulatory adverse selection."27 The social cost of adverse selection is that some risk-averse individuals forego coverage that they would like to purchase at actuarially fair rates.28 To the extent that adverse selection undermines an insurance market entirely (a so-called "death spiral") or leads to strategic insurer efforts to segregate low-risk and high-risk policyholders indirectly by offering multiple coverage options (a separating equilibrium), it can also undermine regulatory objectives to promote subsidization from low-risk to high-risk individuals.29
