Another possibility is that state legislatures pass laws such as this not because they are needed to curb certain behavior on the part of insurers, but because they create the (in these cases, false) impression that legislatures are doing something productive. One can see how such legislation could easily get passed. The insurers would not object, since the laws restrict them from doing something that they do not want to do anyway. Indeed, insurers may support such laws, whose passage may reduce the demand from voters for action on other fronts. The insuring public also would not object, because, as far as they know, these pointless laws are in fact constraining insurers from some socially harmful activity. Who pushes for such legislation? Again, the legislators themselves, who need to give the impression they are doing something, without offending an important constituency. 1 50
